latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Shape Identification:\n   a. Use flood fill algorithm to identify all distinct contiguous shapes of color 8 (sky blue) in the input grid.\n   b. Store each shape as a list of coordinates.\n\n2. Shape Analysis:\n   a. For each shape, calculate:\n      - Size (number of cells)\n      - Bounding box (min and max coordinates)\n      - Aspect ratio (width to height)\n      - Complexity (e.g., number of corners or edges)\n      - Presence of holes\n   b. Determine the shape's type (e.g., line, square, rectangle, L-shape, etc.) based on these characteristics.\n   c. Analyze the shape's position relative to the entire grid (top, middle, bottom, left, right, center).\n\n3. Hierarchical Analysis:\n   a. Identify nested shapes (shapes completely contained within other shapes).\n   b. Create a hierarchy of shapes, with parent shapes and their nested children.\n\n4. Global Pattern Recognition:\n   a. Analyze the overall distribution and arrangement of shapes in the grid.\n   b. Identify any symmetry or repeated patterns.\n\n5. Shape Ranking:\n   a. Develop a ranking system for shapes based on their characteristics (size, complexity, position, etc.).\n   b. Sort shapes from most to least significant according to this ranking.\n\n6. Color Assignment:\n   a. Start with a base set of colors (1: blue, 2: red, 3: green).\n   b. Assign colors to shapes based on their rank and characteristics:\n      - Highest ranked shape gets blue (1)\n      - Second highest gets red (2)\n      - Third highest gets green (3)\n   c. For grids with more than three shapes:\n      - Reuse colors based on shape similarities and hierarchical relationships\n      - Ensure visually distinct shapes have different colors\n      - Consider using additional colors (4 and up) if absolutely necessary\n\n7. Nested Shape Coloring:\n   a. For nested shapes, assign colors that contrast with their parent shape.\n   b. Ensure consistent coloring for similar nested structures across the grid.\n\n8. Color Balance:\n   a. After initial color assignment, analyze the overall color distribution in the grid.\n   b. Adjust colors if necessary to achieve a balanced and visually appealing result.\n\n9. Output Grid Creation:\n   a. Create a new grid with the same dimensions as the input.\n   b. Fill in the background with black (0).\n   c. Color each shape according to the assigned colors.\n\n10. Validation:\n    a. Check that all non-black cells in the input grid have been colored.\n    b. Ensure that the coloring follows the patterns observed in the test cases.\n    c. Verify that the color assignment is consistent for similar shapes and structures across the grid.\n\n11. Refinement:\n    a. If the result doesn't match expected patterns, iterate on the color assignment rules.\n    b. Fine-tune the ranking and coloring algorithms based on specific patterns observed in the test cases.\n\nThis plan addresses the complexities we observed in our reflection, including handling more than three shapes, considering hierarchical relationships, and maintaining consistency across different grid patterns. It also allows for flexibility in color assignment while striving to use the primary colors (1, 2, 3) as much as possible."
